      "title": "Bellman Ford Algorithm",
      "lectureOrder": 266,
      "videoOrder": 264,
      "sourceHash": "cd38907f11d2f8a3fa813f91b8d1966fc1d81fcc3ae47b47bb3c196c1a777c39",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 5.32,
          "end": 8.6,
          "en": "Now here we are going to learn about Bellman-ford algorithm.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 8.6,
          "end": 13.84,
          "en": "Now, Bellman-ford algorithm is also used for solving single source shortest path problem.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 13.84,
          "end": 19.2,
          "en": "But if a graph contains a negative cycle that is reachable from the source, then there is no shortest",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 19.2,
          "end": 19.56,
          "en": "path.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 19.56,
          "end": 25.08,
          "en": "In such cases, Bellman-ford algorithm can detect the negative cycle and report their existence.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 25.12,
          "end": 30.24,
          "en": "An important point over here is whenever we have a negative cycle, we can only report it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 30.24,
          "end": 33.76,
          "en": "We cannot find the shortest path in case of negative cycle.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 33.8,
          "end": 39.68,
          "en": "Now, to understand the Bellman-ford algorithm, let's first look at the graph without negative cycle.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 39.68,
          "end": 42.96,
          "en": "Then we are going to do the same operation for the negative cycle.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 42.96,
          "end": 44.76,
          "en": "So let's say we have a graph like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 44.76,
          "end": 49.28,
          "en": "So if we look at this graph we can easily see that there is a negative weighted edge over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 49.28,
          "end": 51.48,
          "en": "But this is not the negative cycle.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 51.48,
          "end": 55.84,
          "en": "So if you sum up this edge you will see that the result is seven.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 55.84,
          "end": 57.24,
          "en": "So this is a positive number.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 57.28,
          "end": 60.28,
          "en": "Now this graph will be implemented using the adjacency list.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 60.28,
          "end": 63.92,
          "en": "So the adjacency list implementation of this graph is going to be like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 64.24,
          "end": 69.15,
          "en": "So we have the vertices, then we have the edge with the weight.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 69.15,
          "end": 72.27,
          "en": "So I'm just going to put it like this to see how this is going to work.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 72.27,
          "end": 75.78999999999999,
          "en": "Now to find the answer in case of the Bellman-ford algorithm.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 75.83,
          "end": 80.78999999999999,
          "en": "So here again we are going to start from the source vertex and set that distance to zero.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 80.78999999999999,
          "end": 83.39,
          "en": "And all other vertices will be infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 83.39,
          "end": 87.63,
          "en": "So the same logic that we did in case of the Dijkstra's algorithm.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 87.63,
          "end": 90.63,
          "en": "So let's say in this case the source vertex is e.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 91.19,
          "end": 93.63,
          "en": "So for e we are going to set it to zero.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 93.67,
          "end": 96.78999999999999,
          "en": "All other vertices will be set to infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 96.83,
          "end": 100.59,
          "en": "Now in case of Dijkstra's algorithm we were starting from the source vertex.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 100.59,
          "end": 106.99000000000001,
          "en": "But here we are going to run the iteration through the vertices as they are declared in the adjacency",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 106.99000000000001,
          "end": 107.27000000000001,
          "en": "list.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 107.27000000000001,
          "end": 109.71000000000001,
          "en": "So as you can see the first vertex is a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 109.75,
          "end": 113.50999999999999,
          "en": "Then we have b, then we have c, then we have d, then we have e.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 113.55,
          "end": 117.31,
          "en": "So we are going to start to loop through the edge using this order.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 117.31,
          "end": 122.07,
          "en": "So first we are going to start from the edge from A then from B.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 122.11,
          "end": 123.63,
          "en": "And it will continue like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 123.63,
          "end": 126.99,
          "en": "Now the question is how many times that we are going to run the loop.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 126.99,
          "end": 132.94,
          "en": "So in this case the loop will be run V minus one times, where v is the number of the vertices that",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 132.94,
          "end": 134.06,
          "en": "we have in our graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 134.06,
          "end": 136.14,
          "en": "So in our graph we have the vertices.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 136.14,
          "end": 139.46,
          "en": "So this means that we have to run the loop four times.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 139.46,
          "end": 142.86,
          "en": "So in our first iteration first we are going to start from a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 142.9,
          "end": 146.57999999999998,
          "en": "So from a we can go to C and d.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 146.62,
          "end": 149.14,
          "en": "So the distance in A is infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 149.18,
          "end": 153.62,
          "en": "Now if we go to from A to C the edge from A to C is six.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 153.62,
          "end": 156.22,
          "en": "Now infinity plus six is going to be infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 156.22,
          "end": 158.02,
          "en": "So this is not less than infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 158.02,
          "end": 163.74,
          "en": "So we are not going to update the distance of the C because we are going to do the same logic over here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 163.74,
          "end": 166.02,
          "en": "as we did in case of Dijkstra's algorithm.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 166.02,
          "end": 172.26,
          "en": "If the distance in the vertex is less than the new distance, in this case we are going to update that",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 172.26,
          "end": 173.74,
          "en": "distance with the new distance.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 173.74,
          "end": 179.62,
          "en": "So in our case, infinity plus six is infinity which is not less than infinity that we have in the C.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 179.62,
          "end": 181.38,
          "en": "So that's why we are not going to update it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 181.38,
          "end": 184.26,
          "en": "So same thing will be done for the edge from A to D.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 184.3,
          "end": 186.74,
          "en": "So infinity plus six is infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 186.74,
          "end": 188.46,
          "en": "And in D we have infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 188.46,
          "end": 189.74,
          "en": "So it's not less than that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 189.74,
          "end": 191.02,
          "en": "We are not going to update it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 191.02,
          "end": 193.4,
          "en": "That's why we will continue to the next vertex.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 193.4,
          "end": 195.16,
          "en": "Now the next vertex is b.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 195.16,
          "end": 198.44,
          "en": "So as you can see, the next vertex in our adjacency list is b.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 198.44,
          "end": 200.48,
          "en": "So from b we can go to a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 200.52,
          "end": 202.56,
          "en": "So same thing is happening over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 202.56,
          "end": 204.6,
          "en": "Infinity plus three is infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 204.6,
          "end": 205.96,
          "en": "So it's not less than infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 205.96,
          "end": 207.24,
          "en": "We are not going to update it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 207.24,
          "end": 210.24,
          "en": "That's why we will continue to the next vertex which is C.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 210.24,
          "end": 212.12,
          "en": "So same thing we have over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 212.12,
          "end": 214.36,
          "en": "Infinity plus one is infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 214.36,
          "end": 216.72,
          "en": "So it's not less than infinity that we have in the d.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 216.72,
          "end": 218.68,
          "en": "So that's why we are not going to update it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 218.68,
          "end": 220.88,
          "en": "Then we will continue to the next vertex.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 220.88,
          "end": 222.32,
          "en": "Now the next vertex is d.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 222.32,
          "end": 224.2,
          "en": "So from d we go to b.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 224.2,
          "end": 226.92000000000002,
          "en": "So infinity plus negative two is infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 226.92000000000002,
          "end": 229,
          "en": "So we are not going to update the distance.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 229,
          "end": 230.56,
          "en": "So it's going to remain as it is.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 75,
          "start": 230.56,
          "end": 233.24,
          "en": "Then we are continuing to the last vertex which is e.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 76,
          "start": 233.28,
          "end": 235.6,
          "en": "Now from e we can go to B.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 77,
          "start": 235.6,
          "end": 238.4,
          "en": "So it takes four units to go to B.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 78,
          "start": 238.44,
          "end": 240.4,
          "en": "And the distance of E is zero.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 79,
          "start": 240.4,
          "end": 244.04,
          "en": "So zero plus four is four which is less than infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 80,
          "start": 244.04,
          "end": 249.08,
          "en": "That's why the distance of b will be changed to four because it's less than infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 81,
          "start": 249.12,
          "end": 249.84,
          "en": "Now same thing.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 82,
          "start": 249.84,
          "end": 254.24,
          "en": "We are going to do the four edge from E to D zero plus one is one.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 83,
          "start": 254.24,
          "end": 258.31,
          "en": "So the distance in D is infinity, so it is greater than one.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 84,
          "start": 258.31,
          "end": 260.07,
          "en": "So we are going to update it to one.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 85,
          "start": 260.07,
          "end": 263.51,
          "en": "So after first iteration our graph will look like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "start": 263.55,
          "end": 265.99,
          "en": "We can go to a with the infinite number.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 87,
          "start": 265.99,
          "end": 270.67,
          "en": "Because we didn't find the solution from E we can go to be with four unit.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 88,
          "start": 270.67,
          "end": 277.35,
          "en": "So that's why the parent is E because to be we are coming through E and from E to D it is one and the",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 89,
          "start": 277.35,
          "end": 278.47,
          "en": "parent is E.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 90,
          "start": 278.51,
          "end": 280.63,
          "en": "So this is coming from the first iteration.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 91,
          "start": 280.63,
          "end": 282.63,
          "en": "Now we will continue to the second iteration.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 92,
          "start": 282.63,
          "end": 288.27,
          "en": "As we mentioned in case of Bellman-ford algorithm, the iteration will run V minus one time where v",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 93,
          "start": 288.27,
          "end": 289.83,
          "en": "is the number of vertices.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 94,
          "start": 289.83,
          "end": 293.79,
          "en": "So in our case we have five vertices and it's going to be run four times.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 95,
          "start": 293.83,
          "end": 297.75,
          "en": "Now one more time in the next iteration we are going to start from the a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 96,
          "start": 297.79,
          "end": 299.47,
          "en": "From A we can go to C.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 97,
          "start": 299.63,
          "end": 300.95,
          "en": "So nothing changed over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 98,
          "start": 300.95,
          "end": 302.99,
          "en": "So we are not going to update it from A.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 99,
          "start": 303.03,
          "end": 304.07,
          "en": "We can go to D.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 100,
          "start": 304.07,
          "end": 309.07,
          "en": "So here again we are not going to update the distance in D because infinity plus six is infinity which",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 101,
          "start": 309.07,
          "end": 311.43,
          "en": "is greater than the distance in D which is one.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 102,
          "start": 311.43,
          "end": 312.95,
          "en": "That's why we are not going to update it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 103,
          "start": 312.99,
          "end": 314.55,
          "en": "We will continue to be.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 104,
          "start": 314.55,
          "end": 322.46,
          "en": "Now from B we can go to A now in B we have four, and the edge between B to A is three, so four plus",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 105,
          "start": 322.46,
          "end": 324.74,
          "en": "three is seven, which is less than infinity.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 106,
          "start": 324.74,
          "end": 328.5,
          "en": "So this means that the distance in A will be updated to seven.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 107,
          "start": 328.54,
          "end": 330.94,
          "en": "Then we will continue to the next vertex.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 108,
          "start": 330.94,
          "end": 332.78,
          "en": "Now in the next vertex we have c.",
